'''2.''' Next the wind; windward.
{{smaller block|Still you keep o’ the ''windy'' side of the law.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iii. 4. 181.}}}}
'''3.''' Tempestuous; boisterous: as, ''windy'' weather.
{{smaller block|The ''windy'' Seas. ''Heywood'', Hierarchy of Angels, p. 5.}}
'''4.''' Exposed to or affected by the wind.
{{smaller block|The building rook ’ill caw from the ''windy'' tall elm-tree.
{{right|''Tennyson'', May Queen, New-Year’s Eve.}}}}
'''5.''' Wind-like; resembling the wind.
{{smaller block|Her ''windy'' sighs. ''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 51.
<poem>The ''windy'' breath
Of soft petitions. ''Shak.'', K. John, ii. 1. 477.</poem>}}
'''6.''' Tending to generate wind or gas in the stomach; flatulent: as, ''windy'' food.
{{smaller block|This drink is ''windy'', and so is the Fruit [plantain] eaten raw; but boil’d or roasted it is not so.
{{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, I. 314.}}}}
'''7.''' Caused or attended by gas in the stomach or intestines.
{{smaller block|A ''windy'' colic. ''Arbuthnot'', Aliments.}}
'''8.''' Affected with flatulence; troubled with wind in the stomach or bowels. ''Dunglison.''—'''9.''' Airy; unsubstantial; empty; vain.
{{smaller block|What ''windy'' joy this day had I conceived.
{{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 1574.}}
Here’s that ''windy'' applause, that poor transitory pleasure, for which I was dishonoured. ''South.''}}
'''10.''' Talkative; boastful; vain. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|Yet after these blustering insolences and ''windy'' ostentations all this thing is but a man, and that, God knows, a very foolish one. ''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 52.}}

'''windy-footed''' (win′di-fu̇t″ed), ''a.'' Wind-swift; swift-footed. [Rare.]

